INT. PLANETARIUM - DOME HALL - NIGHT

The star projector hums, throwing a slow ocean of light across a curved ceiling. The seats recline. Most are empty.

WINIFRED PARRISH, 30s, a sensible coat and a sensible umbrella, sits alone in row F. She watches Orion rise.

A STRANGER, 30s, in a rumpled raincoat, slides into the seat beside her. He carries a paper cup of coffee and an apologetic smile.

STRANGER
Is this seat taken?

WINIFRED
There are two hundred seats.

STRANGER
I like this one.

She considers him, then looks back at the stars. He sips his coffee. Neither speaks for a while.

WINIFRED
(to herself)
Betelgeuse is late tonight.

STRANGER
It's always late. It's a very old star.

MR. ABERNATHY, 70s, the caretaker in a navy cardigan, shuffles down the aisle with a flashlight. He pauses at row F.

MR. ABERNATHY
Last show's over, folks. Ten minutes.

WINIFRED
Thank you, Mr. Abernathy.

MR. ABERNATHY
Who's your friend?

WINIFRED
(to the Stranger)
Yes. Who are you?

STRANGER
Julian. Julian Hale.

Mr. Abernathy nods, satisfied, and shuffles on.

JULIAN
You come here often?

WINIFRED
Every Thursday.

JULIAN
Then I'll see you Thursday.

She smiles at the ceiling, not at him. Overhead, a comet drifts across the dome.

MR. ABERNATHY
(calling, to the empty hall)
Lights in five!

JULIAN
(softly, to Winifred)
Stay for the comet.

She does.
